  :book-version        "1.0.0"
  :draws-from-preserved (:book "book-of-games-preserved" :ch 1)
  :provenance          ((:source-path "narrative-games-engineer-lane")
                        (:authored-by "Engineer")
                        (:date "2026-07-16"))
  :prose
"Someone told a story. Someone else asked 'and then what did they do?'
The teller shrugged and said 'depends on what you would have done.' That
is where narrative games come from. Not from computers. From a fire and
two people who could not agree on what the hero should do next.

The oldest computer version is Colossal Cave Adventure, 1976, written
partly to teach a nine-year-old daughter how caves worked. It painted no
pictures. It printed a paragraph, waited for a verb, printed the next
paragraph. The whole world was a linked list of rooms and each room was
a paragraph with exits. That is the whole idea. The rest is decoration.

This chapter builds the smallest possible narrative game: one room, two
doors, one choice. The kid types A or B. The game responds. That is it.
Then we teach the physics of a page — text does not slam onto the
screen; it eases in. The previous room does not vanish; it dims. Not
action physics. Patience physics. The physics of turning a page in a
book someone read to you at bedtime and hoped you would remember.

A note on how these examples run. A narrative game paints words on a
surface and waits for a key. In this book we drive the story from a tiny
text console built out of base Motoi — `say` prints a line, and a scene
is just a function that returns the next scene's name. That keeps every
block runnable at the REPL: you can read the whole game as data and
values, without a graphics surface. The memory — Cortex — is real and
wired, and it is the part that matters.

---

## 1.1 The smallest possible narrative game

```scheme
;; A tiny text console: say a line, and drive scenes as plain functions.
(define (say . xs) (for-each display xs) (newline))

;; A kitchen. Two doors. One choice.
(define (kitchen)
  (say \"You are in a small kitchen. A red door stands to the north.\")
  (say \"A blue door stands to the west. Which do you take?\")
  (say \"[A] north (red)   [B] west (blue)\")
  'kitchen)

(define (garden)
  (say \"The red door opens on a garden. Bees.\")
  (say \"You stand in tomatoes to your knees.\")
  'garden)

(define (hallway)
  (say \"The blue door opens on a hallway. Dust.\")
  (say \"A cat sits on the third step of a staircase.\")
  'hallway)

;; The key handler: press A or B, get the next scene.
(define (choose k)
  (cond ((eq? k 'a) (garden))
        ((eq? k 'b) (hallway))
        (else       (kitchen))))

(kitchen)
(choose 'a)
```

That is a game. Not a good game — the choices do not compound, the two
rooms are dead ends, and the kid can loop forever. But the shape is
there. `say` prints a line of prose. Each scene is a function that
describes a room and returns its own name. `choose` is the key handler:
it takes the pressed key and calls the next scene.

Read it out loud to yourself: kitchen, choice, garden or hallway, back
to kitchen. That is the same as `while (playing) { describe; wait;
branch; }` in any other language, except we do not have a while-loop.
Each scene calls the next scene. The stack does the loop for us. This is
the Scheme way — continuations, not iterations.

In a real host with a graphics surface, `say` becomes `paint-text` at
pixel coordinates and `choose` is registered as an on-key handler that
the runtime calls for every keystroke. The shape is identical; only the
output device changes.

---

## 1.2 What the reader sees

When the kid runs `(big-bang :init kitchen)`, the surface goes to a soft
paper color (default for `scene/imagine`). Three lines of text appear.
Nothing moves. The kid presses A. The screen clears. Two new lines
appear. The garden. The kid presses any key. The screen clears again.
Back to the kitchen.

We have not painted a picture. We have painted words. Words are enough.
When someone reads 'you stand in tomatoes to your knees' they see
tomatoes; the graphics card in their head does the rest. Text adventures
are the most graphically intensive games in history — they run on the
best rendering hardware you own, which is your imagination.

The kid may not see this yet. That is fine. In chapter 4 we will add a
character with a body; in chapter 6 we will add a camera. For now, text
is enough. The eleven-year-old who plays this and gets bored is the same
eleven-year-old who reads the book with the missing pages and invents
the ending herself. That kid is our reader.

---

## 1.3 The physics of the page

Slam a paragraph on the screen and it feels rude. Ease it in and it
feels like a story. The alpha of a fading-in line follows an easing
curve — `(easing/apply 'ease-out t)` maps a time `t` in 0..1 to a
smooth 0..1 opacity that arrives fast and settles gently.

```scheme
;; The alpha of a line as it fades in, sampled over its arrival.
(define (fade-alpha t) (easing/apply 'ease-out t))

;; Sample the fade at five moments from start (0.0) to settled (1.0).
(for-each
  (lambda (t)
    (display \"t=\") (display t)
    (display \"  alpha=\") (display (fade-alpha t)) (newline))
  '(0.0 0.25 0.5 0.75 1.0))
```

The alpha rises quickly at first and eases into full opacity — the line
appears, then relaxes onto the page. That is `ease-out`. Try
`'ease-in` for a line that creeps in slowly, or `'spring-ease` for one
that overshoots and bounces once, like a card set down a touch too hard.

Now stagger the arrival of lines so the room assembles itself instead of
appearing all at once:

```scheme
;; Each line arrives at its own moment — first at 0, then 15, 30, 50
;; frames in. We model \"when\" as a schedule of (frame line) pairs.
(define kitchen-lines
  '((0  \"You are in a small kitchen.\")
    (15 \"A red door stands to the north.\")
    (30 \"A blue door stands to the west.\")
    (50 \"[A] north (red)   [B] west (blue)\")))

(for-each
  (lambda (pair)
    (display \"frame \") (display (car pair))
    (display \": \") (display (cadr pair)) (newline))
  kitchen-lines)
```

The schedule staggers the lines. First line at frame 0. Second at frame
15. Third at frame 30. Choices at frame 50. Half a second, one second,
two seconds. The kid reads at a natural pace and the world responds.
This is a lesson she will feel before she can articulate: the game has
time. It is not screaming at her. It is TELLING her a story.

In a graphics host, each entry's frame count is handed to a `world/after`
timer that paints the line with the eased alpha above; a warm off-white
`surface/paper` background stands in for a book page. If we wanted the
text to slide in from the left as well, we would drive its x-position
along the same easing curve (chapter 14 does exactly that for poetry).

Change the fade's timing and it changes the feel: a fast fade snaps the
words in; a slow one makes the kid impatient. Somewhere in the middle is
the sweet spot — fast enough to feel responsive, slow enough to feel
considered.

---

## 1.4 Fading out the old scene

When the kid presses A, the kitchen should not vanish. It should dim.
The garden should fade in on top of the dimmed kitchen, then the kitchen
should clear. Three beats: dim, arrive, clear. Half a second each.

The dim is the fade curve run backwards: brightness eases from 1.0 (full)
down toward a target. We can compute the brightness at each step of the
transition with the same easing verb.

```scheme
;; Brightness as we dim from 1.0 toward a target over the transition.
;; t goes 0..1 across the transition; we ease and interpolate.
(define (dim-brightness target t)
  (let ((e (easing/apply 'ease-out t)))     ;; 0..1, eased
    (+ 1.0 (* e (- target 1.0)))))          ;; 1.0 → target

;; A dim to 50% brightness, sampled across the transition.
(for-each
  (lambda (t)
    (display \"t=\") (display t)
    (display \"  brightness=\") (display (dim-brightness 0.5 t)) (newline))
  '(0.0 0.25 0.5 0.75 1.0))

;; The transition as data: dim the old scene, then move to the next.
(define (goto-scene next-scene-name)
  ;; ... in a host: run the dim above, clear, then enter next-scene ...
  next-scene-name)

(display (goto-scene 'garden)) (newline)
```

`dim-brightness 0.5 t` eases the whole surface from full brightness down
to half over the transition. In a host, the transition runs the dim,
waits for it to finish, clears the surface, and calls the next scene. The
kid sees: kitchen goes gray, screen clears, garden fades in.

Try a target of 0.0 (full black) over a longer transition. The
transition is now slow and total, like a chapter break in a film. Or a
target of 0.9 (barely dim) over a very short one. Now transitions are
almost instant, snappy. Both are valid; they set different rhythms. Fast
transitions for chases. Slow transitions for reflection.

---

## 1.5 The choice as data

Every game we build in this book eventually stores its choices. We start
now, with the simplest possible store: Cortex, Motoi's persistent memory.

```scheme
(define (say . xs) (for-each display xs) (newline))

;; The kitchen offers two doors; the handler REMEMBERS which one was taken.
(define (kitchen-remembered)
  (say \"You are in a small kitchen.\")
  (say \"A red door stands to the north.\")
  (say \"A blue door stands to the west.\")
  (say \"[A] north (red)   [B] west (blue)\")
  'kitchen)

(define (choose-and-remember k)
  (cond
    ((eq? k 'a) (cortex/write 'first-choice \"red\")  (garden-remembered))
    ((eq? k 'b) (cortex/write 'first-choice \"blue\") (hallway-remembered))
    (else       (kitchen-remembered))))

(define (garden-remembered)
  (say \"The garden. Bees. Tomatoes.\")
  (say \"You will remember this door was red.\")
  'garden)

(define (hallway-remembered)
  (say \"The hallway. Dust. A cat.\")
  'hallway)

(kitchen-remembered)
(choose-and-remember 'a)

;; Later — even in a whole new session — the story still knows:
(display (cortex/read 'first-choice)) (newline)              ;; => red
(display (equal? (cortex/read 'first-choice) \"red\")) (newline) ;; => #t
```

`cortex/write 'first-choice \"red\"` stores the value `\"red\"` under the key
`first-choice` in a place that outlives this session. When the kid quits
and comes back tomorrow, `(cortex/read 'first-choice)` still returns
`\"red\"`. That is Cortex — the memory of the story. Chapter 3 is entirely
about it.

One thing to know up front: Cortex saves your values to disk, so a symbol
you write comes back as a **string**. That is why we store `\"red\"` (a
string) and compare with `(equal? … \"red\")`, not `(eq? … 'red)`. Numbers
and booleans round-trip unchanged; symbols and lists-of-symbols come back
as strings. Store strings for anything you will compare later, and you
will never be surprised.

For now, we are just planting the seed. The garden scene tells the kid
'you will remember this door was red' and it is true — the game will
remember it. In three chapters, the shopkeeper is going to say 'ah, I
heard from Mira you came through the red door.' The seed has to be
planted here so it can bloom there.

---

## 1.6 A worked scene: The Kitchen at Dawn

Here is what the shape looks like when we care about the words. Not
placeholder text like 'you are in a small kitchen' — real prose that a
kid or an adult could read out loud.

```scheme
(define (say . xs) (for-each display xs) (newline))

(define (kitchen-at-dawn)
  (say \"It is early. The kitchen has that after-rain quiet. A jar of\")
  (say \"honey has been left on the counter with a spoon in it. Someone\")
  (say \"was here and left in a hurry.\")
  (say \"The RED door leads to the garden. You can hear bees from here.\")
  (say \"The BLUE door leads to the hall. You can hear a cat.\")
  (say \"[A] garden   [B] hall   [C] eat the honey\")
  'kitchen-at-dawn)

(define (dawn-choice k)
  (cond
    ((eq? k 'a)
     (cortex/write 'first-choice \"garden\")
     'garden-at-dawn)
    ((eq? k 'b)
     (cortex/write 'first-choice \"hall\")
     'hall-at-dawn)
    ((eq? k 'c)
     (cortex/write 'first-choice \"honey\")
     (cortex/write 'ate-the-honey #t)
     'the-honey-eating)
    (else 'kitchen-at-dawn)))

(kitchen-at-dawn)
(display (dawn-choice 'c)) (newline)                    ;; => the-honey-eating
(display (cortex/read 'ate-the-honey)) (newline)        ;; => #t
(display (equal? (cortex/read 'first-choice) \"honey\")) (newline)  ;; => #t
```

In a graphics host the three description lines fade in on a stagger and
the choices arrive last (§1.3); here we simply say them in order and let
`dawn-choice` be the key handler.

Look at what changed. We named the room 'kitchen at dawn' instead of
'kitchen' because time of day is part of setting. We put a jar of honey
on the counter because a room needs one specific object the kid can see.
We added a third choice — eat the honey — which is not a door but is
still a valid action. Text adventures teach a truth about narrative:
every scene is a small negotiation between what the writer wants and
what the reader would do.

The honey scene, if the kid picks C, might set two Cortex flags —
`first-choice = honey` and `ate-the-honey = #t`. Fifteen scenes from
now, someone might notice honey on the kid's chin, or the beekeeper
might say 'you smell sweet.' The seed is dropped here; the bloom comes
later.

---

## 1.7 Common desktop tasks — a shopkeeper's cold-open

An operator sells cast-iron cookware. She wants a five-second onboarding
that makes new customers smile before they even see the catalog. She
writes a narrative-game cold-open.

```scheme
(define (say . xs) (for-each display xs) (newline))

;; A tiny narrative scene the shopkeeper serves as her landing page.
(define (shop-cold-open)
  (say \"You walk into a small shop. It smells like beeswax and old fire.\")
  (say \"A woman looks up from a skillet she is seasoning by hand.\")
  (say \"'You looking for a pan, or for a story about a pan?'\")
  (say \"[A] a pan   [B] a story   [C] both\")
  'shop-cold-open)

(define (shop-choice k)
  (cond ((eq? k 'a) 'shop-catalog)
        ((eq? k 'b) 'shop-story)
        ((eq? k 'c) 'shop-both)
        (else       'shop-cold-open)))

(shop-cold-open)
(display (shop-choice 'b)) (newline)   ;; => shop-story
```

In a host, this scene is wrapped in a card the shopkeeper publishes as
her landing page; the card's `:init` runs `shop-cold-open` and registers
`shop-choice` as the key handler. Cards live in the consumer repos, not
in base Motoi — here we show the plain scene the card would wrap.

Three scenes flowing from here — `shop-catalog` opens the actual
listings, `shop-story` tells a two-minute origin story about how the
shopkeeper's grandmother taught her to season cast iron, `shop-both`
does the story first and then the catalog. The operator is doing what
narrative games have always done: welcoming a stranger, giving them a
choice, treating them like a guest.

This works because the operator has a voice. Her shop is not 'a shop.'
It is a small shop that smells like beeswax and old fire. Every operator
who writes a cold-open should write it in her actual voice — the voice
she uses when a friend walks in the door. The runtime will help her tune the
prose if she asks (chapter 12), but the voice has to be hers.

---

## 1.8 Routing patterns — one page, three shapes

Not every narrative game is a graph of rooms. Sometimes it is a stream
of scenes with no branches at all. Sometimes it is a wide fan of one-shot
scenes off a hub. Three shapes to know from the start:

**The corridor.** Scene 1 leads only to scene 2, which leads only to
scene 3. No branches. Use this when you are telling a story and the kid
is a reader, not a driver. Poems (chapter 14) live here.

**The hub.** One central room with N choices, each of which returns you
to the hub after playing out. Use this for museums, shops, and any place
where the kid is meant to explore rather than progress. Our kitchen with
its two return-here doors is a two-spoke hub.

**The branch.** Every choice leads to a new node with its own choices.
Use this for stories with real stakes. Chapter 2 formalizes this shape
as the story tree.

Most narrative games are a mix. A branch story with a corridor prologue
and a hub-shaped shop in the middle is completely normal. Your job as a
designer is to pick the right shape for each moment.

---

## 1.9 Closing

You have written a game. Not a big one. But a game. A kitchen, two
doors, a choice. The text eases in. The scene dims when you leave. The
choice is stored in a place that will remember it tomorrow.

Change one thing. Add a third door. Or change the kitchen to a bedroom
at 3am. Or write a fourth scene and wire a new key into the handler. You
know a handful of pieces now — `say`, a scene as a function, a key
handler with `cond`, an easing curve, and `cortex/write` /
`cortex/read`. That is enough to make something someone else has never
seen.

The next chapter formalizes what we did into a story tree — a data
structure the engine reads, not a hand-written cascade of functions. The
kid designs the tree in her head, types it out as nested lists, and the
engine walks it. That is when narrative games start to feel like the
real thing.

For now, run the kitchen. Watch the text arrive. Press A. Press B. Play
your own game for a minute. That is the whole point.")
